Due to what appears to be a code generation issue, libttf2=20
1.4pre.20011029-1 and XFree86 4.2 segfault on Alpha when attempting to=20
render some TrueType fonts.

I have not fully traced this code gen error to the source that triggers it=
=20
in libttf2, but it appears to be similar to the one in gtk+ where adjacent=
=20
'short' fields in a struct get corrupted when writing into one or the=20
other.  You can reproduce it by building the source for libttf2 and=20
running the example programs (I used 'ftview 12 Arial.ttf').

Compiling libttf2 without -O2 (without any -O flag) on Alpha works around=
=20
this problem, as does compiling with gcc-3.0.  I would recommend to the=20
libttf2 maintainer to follow one of these courses of action to close this=
=20
bug. (I'll forward this message to the bug).

The situation in XFree86 is more difficult.  This bug is pretty major for=
=20
X because any attempt to use an affected TrueType font causes the server=20
to exit.  This bug has been present for a long time (certainly since=20
XFree86 4.1) and is still present in 4.2.  X uses the freetype sources=20
(major version 1, now renamed libttf2 by Debian) directly, building pieces=
=20
of the library into the libfreetype.a extension module, rather than=20
relying on the library from the package.  I can see four ways around the=20
bug for X:

* Modify this extension's build procedure, on Alpha, to not use=20
optimization.  We certainly don't want to compile the entire X server=20
without optimization.
* Build X with gcc-3.0.  I have built the latest experimental XFree86 4.2=
=20
packages with gcc-3.0 and they do fix this problem, and seem to work OK.
* Get X to switch to freetype 2 (libfreetype6 on Debian).  This is a=20
non-trivial change, as the API was completely rewritten.  Freetype 2 also=
=20
has problems on Alpha (e.g. misrendered glyphs) but I have not yet looked=
=20
into them.
* Find the code that triggers this code gen bug and modify it to make the=
=20
compiler happy.  This approach turned out to be unworkable for gtk+ on=20
Alpha because there were other similar failures.

Comments?

-Doug


